					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/tunedtoadeadchannel" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(304457); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=304457&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608930980" alt="tunedtoadeadchannel" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=304457)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/tunedtoadeadchannel" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(304457); return false;" >tunedtoadeadchannel</a></span> <span class="odate time_1319498191 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">24 Oct 2011 23:16</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1289470">
									

<p>This is really, really well written. On a technical and storytelling level, it's more or less perfect, barring the misplaced modifier I just fixed.</p>
<p>The problem is I can't fit a superpowered cyborg agent into my view of the current Foundation. A 212 modified hypercapable agent, sure, but there's a difference between being able to runjumpclimb really well and being able to dislocate all your bones, turn one hand into a blade, dislocate your jaw and change the shape of your teeth, and turn the other hand into a smooth hook.</p>
<p>In this story, kramer's not an agent. She's a straight up SCP. If you'd <em>said</em> she was an SCP, it would be fine, but as it is you imply that super ninja robot agents are a normal thing for the Foundation, and I can't upvote that, because it ruins the horror of the concept.</p>
<p>Normal humans doing their best against strange and terrible things = scary. Supervillain ninja cyborg shapeshifters doing their best against strange and terrible things = a disappointing eighties saturday morning cartoon.</p>
<p>Downvoted. Sorry, gears.</p>

							</div>

								
